{{also|épicycle}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|epicyclus}}, from {{uder|en|grc|ἐπίκυκλος}}, from {{m|grc|ἐπί||upon}} + {{m|grc|κύκλος||circle}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛpɪˌsaɪkəl/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-epicycle.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|astronomy}} A small [[circle]] whose centre is on the [[circumference]] of a larger circle; in Ptolemaic astronomy it was seen as the basis of revolution of the "seven planets", given a fixed central Earth.
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes}}, Folio Society, 2006, vol.1, p.155:
#*: Is it not [Philosophie], that{{...}}teacheth miserie, famine and sicknesse to laugh? Not by reason of some imaginarie '''Epicicles''', but by naturall and palpable reasons.
# {{lb|en|geometry}} Any circle whose circumference [[roll]]s around that of another circle, thus creating a [[hypocycloid]] or [[epicycloid]].
# {{lb|en|organic chemistry}} A [[ring]] of [[atom]]s joining parts of an already [[cyclic]] compound
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} An [[ad hoc]] [[complication]] added to a [[model]] to make it fit the known [[data]]
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|title=The Journal of the Siam Society|volume=66-67|page=152
|passage=If two chronicles seemed contradictory, instead of trying to choose between them, a rationalization ('''epicycle''') was devised to cover both.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1998|author=Paul Joseph Kelly|title=Impartiality, Neutrality and Justice: Re-reading Brian Barry’s Justice as Impartiality|page=12
|passage=Rather than solve the theoretical problem of how to produce a method of political ethics, the contractarian device introduces an unnecessary theoretical '''epicycle''' into what is otherwise a coherent account of social justice in particular and political morality in general.}}

